an Resource Management (BS) Justice Administration Law Enforcement Management Medical Laboratory Science MLS 2+2, MLS Post-Baccalaureate Nursing RN-BSN, RN-MSN, RN-MSN/MBA Psychology Rehabilitation Studies Social Work (BSW) Graduate Degrees Business Administration (MBA) Counseling Addictions, School Counseling, Mental Health, Rehabilitation Education Curriculum & Instruction, Educational Leadership, Elementary, Middle Grades, Secondary, Teacher Leadership, Master of Arts in Teaching (Initial Certification), Special Education Nursing MSN, MSN-MBA, RN-MSN, RN-MSN/MBA TU Honors Cost Tuition and Fees About Financial Aid Apply for Financial Aid Financial Aid Eligibility Managing Your Financial Aid Scholarships Military Tuition/Veterans Active Duty, Guard, Reserves, Veterans, DoD Civilians and military spouses Corporate Partners Student Accounts Student Life Campus Life Dining Services Housing Student Activities Safety & Security Student IDs Community Discounts Community Resources Registrar Catalog Student Handbook Course Schedule Transcripts Apply for Graduation Commencement TU Library & Learning Commons About the Library Library Policies Library FAQs Services Bookstore & Spirit Shop Book lists Bookstore Online Spirit Shop Student Support Center Career Center Disability Services Student Success Advising Tutoring Veterans Services Student Veterans of America Veterans Services and Career Transition Center Student Technology Assistance HawkLink, Email, Canvas, Password Reset, Proctor U Athletics Alumni & Friends University Advancement Meet the Team Your Gift Matters Giving Societies and Circles Endowments Impact Reports Donate Alumni Get Involved Alumni News & Events Upcoming Events Veterans Resource Fair Cairo Scholarship Soirée Dining for Education Community Resources Public & Media Relations Genealogical Library Pre-College Programs Veterans Services Ways to Give Woodlands 100 TU Annual Fund Events & Sponsorships Planned Giving About History Consumer Information Accreditation and Approvals State Authorizations Institutional Review Board Employment Faculty/Staff Technology Resources Public & Media Relations Sexual Misconduct Policy Title IX Contact Us Campus Maps Strategic Plan President's Welcome Home > Library Assistant Work Study Library Assistant Work Study Function : Library Assistant-Access Services is a work-study position and applicant must be eligible and approved through Financial Aid.  Library Assistants must report to the Library Director, or other designated professional librarian on a daily basis, or as indicated by a prescribed schedule, for all shift assignments. Responsibilities : Maintains presence at the circulation desk to greet patrons, answer directional questions, and answer the phone adhering to customer service standards Assists patrons in locating, utilizing, and/or checking out library resources Operates the library circulation and Interlibrary Loan systems Assists patrons in use of library computers,printers, and copiersand with basic technical questions Checks the book drop and picks up the library mail Records number of patrons entering the library Performs routine cleaning and straightening of furniture Prepare and execute Library displays Updates Library bulletin boards Performs other duties as assigned by the immediate supervisor or other staff members Special roles include: managing the library social media accounts by making and posting flyers, events, and posts; planning and executing events and activities to take place in the library; other roles assigned as needed. Reports To :  Library Director Qualifications : Must be federal work study eligible. To determine eligibility please complete the Jotform at: https://form.jotform.com/202784938888175 . Library Assistant-Access Services should have average eye-hand coordination, use of fingers, and good verbal and written communication skills. They should be able to work with detail, check accuracy, make visual comparisons, and work well with people. Library Assistant-Access Services should also be able to move often from one task to another using several different skills. They should have the ability to walk, stand, sit, push, pull, bend, stoop and reach for long periods of time as well as lift up to 35 pounds.  Work Study Assistants should have experience and skill in the operation of personal computers, word processing, e-mail, and searching the Internet. Terms of Employment: Part-Time Federal Work Study Position-Hours not to exceed 10 per week WORK ETHICS: Enhances the mission of the university by demonstrating willingness to work as a team player Complies with the policies and procedures of the university and the library Maintains confidentiality Arrives at work punctually for all shifts and informs supervisor when unable to attend or if late Works a flexible schedule as needed and understands that evening work is expected Gives priority to library duties over personal endeavors that may be allowed (homework, etc.) Demonstrates professionalism in contact with students, coworkers and the public Displays cooperative, supportive and professional behavior toward colleagues and the administration Recognizes and respects the line between professional and personal relationships with students Demonstrates willingness to be of service and exhibits pleasant and courteous behavior toward customers (students, parents, employers, media), organizational coworkers and other public contacts To be considered for this position, please complete the application at https://apply.appone.com/job/6a8ccc896a3e5834ac7c0cd3 . Thomas University Education that Engages ...Empowers... Transforms ™ 1550 Magnolia Street Thomasville, GA 31792 229.226.1621 Contact Us Peoples Bookstore & Spirit Shop HawkLink Student Accounts Financial Aid Campus Public & Media Relations Employment University Privacy Policy Consumer Information Website Feedback Follow Us thomas/1950/ @ThomasU1950 thomasuniversity ThomasUniversityTU © 2026 Thomas University. All Rights Reserved. Website developed and hosted by Pen Publishing Interactive"
